  "review_notes": [
    "The italic there is is the construction-supplied presentative frame of the verbless clause and joins עֵת's unit rather than being added (rule 20).",
    "one before הָאָדָם is a contrastive determiner standing where the phrase's the would stand, so the article morpheme takes it (rule 41); both marginal alternatives were read and set aside.",
    "The לוֹ of לְרַע לוֹ is read under rule 40 rather than fused under rules 30 and 49: the ASV spends no English word on the dative relation, since his is the word a bare suffix takes in any possessive context, and rule 49's fused genitive governs a לְ that is itself a verbless clause's predicate. This matches the same construction at 8.7 and 8.15; the fused reading is the competing one."
